# Intake natural-language-handling fix — apply ladder

Fixes the FOUR natural-language-handling bugs the loam 1.0 acceptance
smoke caught in the onboarding intake
(`docs/experiments/loam-1.0-acceptance-smoke-run.md`, top-line
NOT-READY). All four live in a single file:
`framework/workspace-bootstrap/src/loam/workspace_bootstrap/translate_in_intake.py`.

Sub-plan: `docs/plans/intake-nl-handling-fix.md`.

Scope: single-component fence on `framework/workspace-bootstrap/`.
Composes on the N3 translate-in intake; edits no other component.

AC families (each fix → a NAMED AC + a test on the natural-language case):

  - AC.INTAKE-ECHO.1 — the proposal DISTILLS the stop/start reply to a
    short intent phrase (`_distill_intent`): leading conversational
    filler ("Oh, that's an easy one —") is peeled, the first content
    clause is taken, trailing emphatic filler ("… is killing me") is
    dropped, and the phrase is capped at a bounded word budget. A reply
    that is already a short phrase passes through unchanged. The raw
    multi-sentence reply no longer appears verbatim in the proposal or
    the opt-in offer (the Bug-1 garble).
  - AC.INTAKE-AFFIRM.1 — `_is_yes`/`_is_no` match the first WORD-TOKEN
    with surrounding punctuation stripped (`_first_token`), so
    "Yeah, that'd actually help" / "yes, basically!" / "Sure." read as
    affirmations and the deep-research opt-in gate fires (the
    AC.SMOKE.3 gate failure). A reply whose first token is neither an
    affirmation nor a negation still routes to the correction branch,
    so substantive rewrites ("actually I want X") replace the seed.
  - AC.INTAKE-VACUUM.1 — `_looks_empty` adds normalised-token regexes
    robust to inserted adverbs ("I don't EVEN know where to start").
    The negated-knowledge tell is LEAD-sensitive (only the opening
    clause) so a substantive role description with a late aside
    ("…I couldn't tell you which one I'd hand off") is NOT misread as
    empty; unambiguous tells (where-to-start / just-do-my-job) match
    anywhere.
  - AC.INTAKE-ROLE.1 — `_extract_role_noun` reduces a multi-sentence
    role description ("I'm a paralegal at a small litigation firm — …")
    to the role NOUN ("paralegal") before the `{role}` substitution in
    `_leverage_from_role`, the seeded objective text, the slug, and the
    deep-research call. A bare title ("civil engineer") passes through.

Method-level choices (builder's call per ODD §1.1):

  - `_is_yes`/`_is_no` use first-word-token matching (the report's
    named fix shape). No test or report evidence required a "no, X"
    reply to route to the correction branch; a reply LEADING with an
    affirmation/negation is a confirm/reject, and the correction branch
    is reached when the first token is neither.
  - The idea-vacuum regexes are split LEAD-sensitive vs ANYWHERE to
    avoid the over-match where a rich role description containing
    "couldn't tell you which one" was wrongly classified empty.
  - `_distill_intent` is deterministic (clause split + filler peeling +
    word-budget cap), not an LLM call — the report offered an LLM
    intent-classify as an option; the deterministic distillation is
    sufficient for the observed failures and keeps the intake
    dependency-free.
  - Tests = 1 NEW file (6 tests across the 4 AC families) driving the
    VERBATIM raw replies from the smoke transcript; 23 existing
    intake-family tests pass unchanged.

